  "return_code": 0,
  "success": true,
  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) has led to the emergence of code agents—autonomous systems capable of generating, executing, and debugging code. While these agents offer tremendous potential for software development workflows, they also introduce significant safety and security challenges. This paper presents a comprehensive survey of recent research advances in code agents and their associated red-teaming methodologies. We examine the growing need for robust safety evaluation frameworks, highlighting key contributions that address the vulnerabilities inherent in code-generating AI systems. We discuss several seminal works including RedCodeAgent, which introduces automated red-teaming for diverse code agents; RedCode, which provides a comprehensive benchmark for assessing risky code execution and generation; and DecodingTrust, which offers a broad assessment of trustworthiness in GPT models. Our analysis reveals critical gaps in current safety evaluation practices and outlines promising directions for future research in securing code agents.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent one of the most significant applications of large language models in recent years. These systems combine natural language understanding with code generation capabilities, enabling them to perform complex programming tasks autonomously. From writing functions to debugging errors and generating entire software modules, code agents have revolutionized software development workflows.\n\nHowever, this powerful capability comes with substantial risks. Code agents are often integrated with code interpreters and execution environments, which means they can potentially execute malicious or harmful code. The ability of these agents to dynamically generate and run code introduces unique security vulnerabilities that traditional software safety measures may not adequately address.\n\nThe growing concern over code agent safety has spurred a wave of research into red-teaming methodologies—systematic approaches to identifying vulnerabilities and weaknesses in these systems. Red-teaming involves simulating adversarial attacks to uncover security flaws before malicious actors can exploit them. In the context of code agents, this is particularly challenging due to the dynamic nature of code execution and the complex interaction patterns between agents and their environments.\n\nThis paper surveys recent advances in code agent safety and red-teaming, examining key contributions that have shaped our understanding of these systems' vulnerabilities. We focus on three pivotal works that address different aspects of code agent safety: automated red-teaming frameworks, benchmark datasets for evaluation, and comprehensive trustworthiness assessments.\n\n## Related Work\n\nThe field of code agent safety has evolved rapidly, with researchers developing increasingly sophisticated methodologies for identifying and mitigating risks. We examine three seminal contributions that have significantly advanced this area.\n\n### Automated Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\nOne of the most significant advances in code agent safety is the introduction of automated red-teaming frameworks. RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents [1] presents a groundbreaking approach to systematically uncovering vulnerabilities in code agents. This work introduces an adaptive memory module that leverages existing jailbreak knowledge to dynamically select the most effective red-teaming strategies. The authors develop simulated sandbox environments to evaluate code execution results, addressing limitations of LLM-based judges that rely solely on static code analysis. Their comprehensive evaluation across multiple state-of-the-art code agents demonstrates significantly improved attack success rates compared to existing methods.\n\nThe RedCodeAgent framework is particularly notable for its ability to adapt its attack strategies based on learned patterns and successfully identify vulnerabilities that might otherwise remain hidden. By automating the red-teaming process, this approach enables scalable and efficient safety assessments of code agents, which is crucial given the rapid development cycle of these systems.\n\n### Benchmarking Code Agent Safety\n\nComprehensive benchmarks are essential for evaluating the safety of code agents in a standardized and reproducible manner. RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents [2] addresses this need by providing a comprehensive evaluation framework focused on risky code scenarios. This work introduces a benchmark that specifically targets the safety concerns arising from code agents' ability to generate and execute potentially harmful code.\n\nThe RedCode benchmark covers a diverse range of risk categories, enabling researchers to systematically evaluate code agents across different types of safety threats. By providing standardized evaluation protocols and datasets, this work facilitates more rigorous comparison between different code agent implementations and safety measures. The benchmark's focus on both code generation and execution aspects provides a holistic assessment of safety risks in real-world usage scenarios.\n\n### Trustworthiness Assessment of Language Models\n\nWhile code-specific safety is crucial, it is equally important to assess the broader trustworthiness of the underlying language models. DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models [3] provides an extensive evaluation framework that goes beyond code-specific concerns. This work assesses multiple dimensions of trustworthiness including toxicity, stereotype bias, adversarial robustness, privacy concerns, and machine ethics.\n\nThe DecodingTrust framework reveals critical vulnerabilities in GPT models that have implications for code agents built upon these foundations. For instance, the authors found that models can be easily misled to generate toxic and biased outputs, and that they may leak private information from training data or conversation history. These findings underscore the importance of comprehensive trustworthiness evaluation in developing safe and reliable code agents.\n\nThe correlation between general model trustworthiness and code agent safety highlights the need for evaluation frameworks that consider both domain-specific and general safety aspects. As code agents become more sophisticated and integrate with increasingly complex environments, addressing these broader trustworthiness concerns becomes critical.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe emergence of code agents has opened new frontiers in software development while simultaneously introducing significant safety and security challenges. This paper has surveyed key research advances in code agent red-teaming and safety evaluation, highlighting the critical contributions of RedCodeAgent [1], RedCode [2], and DecodingTrust [3].\n\nOur analysis reveals several important insights. First, automated red-teaming approaches like RedCodeAgent represent a necessary evolution from manual safety testing, enabling scalable and adaptive security assessments. Second, comprehensive benchmarks such as RedCode are essential for standardizing safety evaluation and facilitating meaningful comparisons between different approaches. Third, the broader trustworthiness assessment framework provided by DecodingTrust reminds us that code agent safety is deeply connected to the general reliability and safety of the underlying language models.\n\nDespite these advances, significant challenges remain. The rapidly evolving nature of code agent capabilities necessitates continuous adaptation of safety evaluation methods. Future work should focus on developing more sophisticated red-teaming frameworks that can anticipate emerging vulnerabilities, creating more comprehensive benchmarks that cover edge cases and novel attack vectors, and integrating trustworthiness assessment directly into the code agent development lifecycle.\n\nAs code agents become increasingly integrated into critical software development workflows and infrastructure management, ensuring their safety and reliability must remain a top priority. The research community must continue to develop robust evaluation methodologies and safety frameworks to keep pace with the rapid advancement of these powerful systems.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, Zhao-Run Chen, Zi-Nan Lin, Xander Davies, Yarin Gal, D. Song, and Bo Li. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie, et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv preprint arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[3] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, Chulin Xie, Mintong Kang, Chenhui Zhang, Che-Jian Xu, Zidi Xiong, Ritik Dutta, Rylan Schaeffer, Sang T. Truong, Simran Arora, Mantas Mazeika, Dan Hendrycks, Zinan Lin, Yuk-Kit Cheng, O. Koyejo, D. Song, and Bo Li. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv preprint arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\nEOF"
}'}

